= Self-modeling agent — Simple =

A '''self-modeling agent''' is an agent that keeps and uses information about itself.

Its self-model might describe:

* what tools it has;
* what it can and cannot do;
* its current internal state;
* its goals;
* its physical structure;
* how reliable its own behavior has been.

A world model is about the environment. A self-model is about the agent itself.

A self-model is also not the same as identity. Identity asks which persistent agent this is. A self-model asks what the agent represents about itself.

Robotics research has shown machines that model their own bodies and update those models after damage so they can recover behavior.

Self-modeling does not imply consciousness.

